<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

$q = static fn(string $difficulty, string $stem, string $answer, array $distractors, string $explanation, string $competency, string $scenario): array => [
    'difficulty'=>$difficulty, 'question'=>$stem, 'answer'=>$answer, 'distractors'=>$distractors,
    'explanation'=>$explanation, 'tested_competency'=>$competency, 'scenario'=>$scenario,
];

return [
    'cae-cultural-context-and-artistic-evidence'=>[
        $q('easy','A class views a contemporary sculpture with repeated curved forms and open spaces. Which first response is grounded in artistic evidence?','Describe the repeated curves and open spaces before proposing what the work may communicate.',['State that the sculpture represents the whole community where it was displayed.','Assume the artist intended a specific message without consulting any source.','Rank the sculpture by how closely it resembles a familiar object.'],'Formal description identifies observable features; interpretation should be distinguished from unsupported claims about meaning.','Separate artistic observation from contextual interpretation.','sculpture-description'),
        $q('medium','Two learners interpret the same image differently and each points to details in the composition. What should the teacher ask?','How does each visible feature support the interpretation, and what context could help refine it?',['Which learner should replace their view with the teacher’s opinion.','Which interpretation sounds more confident regardless of evidence.','Whether the class can choose one meaning by majority vote.'],'More than one interpretation may be defensible when learners connect it to evidence and relevant context.','Support evidence-based interpretation and respectful comparison.','different-image-interpretations'),
        $q('medium','A student says an artwork must be joyful because it uses bright colors. What is the best critique prompt?','Which features, including color relationships and composition, support or complicate that reading?',['Assume bright color signals happiness without considering composition or cultural context.','Which artist used the most colors in the image.','Can the student replace the interpretation with a fixed definition.'],'The prompt encourages close looking and avoids assuming a single emotional meaning from one feature.','Analyze how artistic choices contribute to meaning.','bright-color-interpretation'),
        $q('medium','A teacher wants learners to discuss the social meaning of a song performance. Which preparation is most sound?','Use the performance as evidence and add reliable context about its source, purpose, and setting.',['Infer the performance’s full history from the clothing alone.','Treat one performer’s view as the only valid interpretation.','Avoid contextual research because music can only be judged by sound.'],'The PRC scope includes art as social and cultural expression; context should be sourced and bounded.','Combine artistic and contextual evidence.','song-performance-context'),
        $q('medium','Learners compare two artworks from different periods. What should be the basis for a defensible comparison?','Specific choices in each work together with reliable evidence about their contexts.',['A claim that all artists in each period used the same style.','Which work is more popular with the class.','A list of dates without reference to the works themselves.'],'Comparison uses both features of the works and relevant contextual evidence without overgeneralizing.','Compare works with formal and contextual evidence.','cross-period-comparison'),
        $q('hard','A source gives limited information about the maker and use of a community artwork. How should learners frame an interpretation?','State what can be observed, identify what the source confirms, and mark broader claims as unresolved.',['Fill in the missing history using a familiar stereotype.','Assume the artwork represents every member of the community.','Avoid describing the artwork because its context is incomplete.'],'Clear limits make an interpretation more accurate; missing context does not prevent careful observation.','Bound claims when contextual evidence is incomplete.','limited-artwork-context'),
    ],
    'cae-regional-identity-and-artifact-study'=>[
        $q('easy','A teacher displays one locally made craft and calls it the standard design of the entire region. What is the strongest revision?','Identify its documented maker and context, then invite comparison with other sourced works rather than treating it as representative.',['Keep the label because one local object is enough to define a region.','Remove all local works and use only international examples.','Ask learners to copy the design before discussing where it came from.'],'One artifact cannot represent all makers or communities; contextual attribution and comparison avoid flattening diversity.','Study specific regional works without overgeneralization.','single-regional-craft'),
        $q('medium','Learners compare two artworks associated with different local communities. Which task best develops cultural literacy?','Compare material, form, and documented context while noting differences within and across communities.',['Choose one work as more authentically Filipino than the other.','Assume both communities use the same symbols because they are nearby.','Memorize the region names without studying the artworks.'],'Specific comparison respects community distinctions and avoids ranking authenticity.','Compare local works with accurate contextual distinctions.','two-local-works'),
        $q('medium','A learner says a regional art form has not changed over time. How can the teacher prompt evidence-based revision?','Compare documented examples from different dates and ask what continuity and change are visible.',['Treat one documented version as timeless and disregard evidence of later change.','Assume every contemporary version is less authentic.','Ask learners to choose which example is oldest by appearance alone.'],'The curriculum includes conventional, contemporary, emerging, and sustainable practices; evidence can show continuity and change.','Trace artistic change without ranking authenticity.','regional-art-change'),
        $q('medium','A public museum label identifies an artwork’s maker and origin, while a social media caption gives a conflicting claim. What should learners do?','Compare the claims with reliable source information and state which details remain supported.',['Use the social media claim because it is more recent.','Combine both claims into one account without checking.','Choose the claim that best fits a familiar story about the region.'],'Source comparison supports accurate interpretation and prevents unsourced generalization.','Evaluate source quality for cultural context.','conflicting-art-labels'),
        $q('medium','A student wants to present a community performance as a single uniform tradition. Which teaching prompt is most helpful?','What evidence shows who performs it, in what context, and whether the source describes variation?',['Why must one example speak for every community member.','Which costume best symbolizes the whole country.','Can the student replace the presentation with a national flag.'],'Specific questions about practice and context help preserve variation rather than flattening a tradition.','Represent cultural diversity with bounded claims.','uniform-performance-claim'),
        $q('hard','A class has time to study only one artwork from a diverse community. How can the teacher avoid implying that it is the only or definitive example?','Frame it as one documented work and explicitly note what additional sources or community perspectives would broaden the study.',['Present it as the complete account because the class has limited time.','Avoid naming its source so learners do not ask about other examples.','Ask learners to invent details to make the study feel complete.'],'Transparent scope shows what the chosen work can teach and where further perspectives are needed.','Communicate the limits of a single case study.','one-artwork-time-limit'),
    ],
    'cae-ethical-community-engagement'=>[
        $q('easy','A teacher finds an online image of a community design with no creator or context listed. What is the best next step before using it as a model?','Seek a reliable source and appropriate community context, or choose a documented alternative.',['Use the image because public access means every use is appropriate.','Ask learners to copy the design before checking its source.','Describe it as traditional without identifying the community.'],'Public availability does not establish context or appropriate use; documented sourcing protects accurate and respectful teaching.','Verify source and context before teaching a cultural design.','unattributed-community-image'),
        $q('medium','A community arts educator tells the class that a particular practice should not be reproduced outside its intended context. What should the teacher do?','Respect that guidance and plan a different activity using material clearly shared for classroom learning.',['Ask learners to reproduce the practice but avoid naming its source.','Treat the guidance as one opinion and continue with the planned copy exercise.','Use a similar design from another community instead.'],'The RA 8371 recognizes cultural and intellectual rights; a respectful lesson follows the stated boundary rather than substituting another tradition.','Respect community guidance about cultural material.','restricted-practice-guidance'),
        $q('medium','Learners will create work inspired by a living tradition discussed with a community knowledge holder. What should the teacher establish?','Clarify the learning purpose, attribution, what material may be shared, and how the community wishes to be represented.',['Assume one conversation authorizes every future classroom use.','Ask learners to imitate the form without explaining the source.','Present the tradition as anonymous so the lesson remains neutral.'],'Ethical engagement requires attribution and attention to the source’s stated scope, not assumed blanket permission.','Plan responsible engagement with cultural knowledge.','community-knowledge-holder'),
        $q('medium','A student’s artwork uses a motif copied from an online image. Which critique question supports ethical practice?','Who made the source image, what context is documented, and how will the student acknowledge or transform the source appropriately?',['How can the student hide the copied source from classmates.','Why should every online image be treated as free of attribution.','Which copied motif will earn the most points.'],'Attribution and context are part of responsible arts work; the teacher can guide original transformation.','Address attribution in creative work.','online-motif-source'),
        $q('medium','A lesson uses one artist to introduce a diverse cultural community. What addition best improves representation?','Include additional sourced perspectives and let learners compare the artist’s individual view with broader evidence.',['Describe the artist as speaking for everyone in the community.','Remove the artist’s name and present the work as generic.','Ask learners to decide what the entire community believes.'],'An artist’s work is an individual perspective; additional evidence prevents unsupported generalization.','Distinguish an individual artist from community-wide representation.','single-artist-representation'),
        $q('hard','A student proposes a performance based on a sacred practice but cannot find a source showing it is intended for public performance. What is the most responsible response?','Pause the plan, seek appropriate guidance, and choose a source explicitly intended for public performance.',['Treat classroom use as permitted because the purpose is educational, despite the missing context.','Ask a public web forum whether the material is shared, then proceed if users agree.','Recreate the style from a different community’s similar practice instead.'],'Uncertainty about use should be resolved with appropriate guidance; a different public source is a sound alternative. The replacement must itself be documented and suitable for classroom use.','Respond ethically when cultural use is uncertain.','uncertain-performance-use'),
    ],
    'cae-material-and-medium-selection'=>[
        $q('easy','Learners want to build a stable sculpture with lightweight recycled materials. Which planning step comes first?','Test how the available materials join and balance before selecting the final construction method.',['Choose a display location before checking whether the sculpture stands.','Require every learner to use the same material regardless of the design.','Assess only the sculpture’s color palette.'],'Material behavior affects stability and technique, so an early test supports an intentional process.','Select a medium with its properties and purpose in mind.','recycled-sculpture-material'),
        $q('medium','A learner wants to communicate movement in a still image. Which medium decision is most relevant?','Choose compositional, line, or repeated-shape techniques that can suggest motion in the selected medium.',['Use the most expensive available material.','Select a medium only because it is familiar to the teacher.','Add unrelated decoration until the image looks complete.'],'A medium and technique should serve the intended effect. The learner can test how line direction, repeated forms, and composition imply movement, then choose the treatment that makes the idea clearest.','Match artistic process to expressive intent.','still-image-motion'),
        $q('medium','A class plans a sound collage but has limited recording equipment. What is a sound design response?','Choose available safe sound sources and plan how their sequence and contrast will communicate the idea.',['Cancel sound creation because professional equipment is unavailable.','Record any sounds without considering the intended message.','Use a prerecorded song and claim it as the class’s original work.'],'Creative choices can use accessible materials while preserving intention, attribution, and safe practice.','Select appropriate accessible media and processes.','limited-recording-equipment'),
        $q('medium','A learner’s clay form cracks during drying. Which teacher response best supports material understanding?','Review thickness, joins, and drying conditions, then invite a small test before revising the final form.',['Tell the learner the design failed and must be discarded.','Change the project to painting without examining the clay.','Ask the learner to hide the crack before reflection.'],'The material response provides evidence for an informed revision and future process choice.','Use material behavior to guide revision.','clay-drying-crack'),
        $q('medium','A digital illustration uses a tool that produces an unintended flat effect. What should the learner try?','Test another tool or layer treatment and compare how each changes depth and meaning.',['Keep the first setting because digital tools cannot be revised.','Ask the teacher to select every digital setting.','Add more effects without comparing their impact.'],'Experimenting and comparing tools helps the learner make deliberate choices.','Use medium-specific tools experimentally.','digital-flat-effect'),
        $q('hard','A large group must create a performance with a small accessible stage. Which medium and process decision is strongest?','Plan movement scale, pathways, sound, and roles around the space while preserving the intended expressive idea.',['Keep the original staging and ask performers to squeeze into the available space.','Remove performers who need more space without adapting the design.','Replace the performance with a written report.'],'Medium and staging choices should fit site conditions and access while retaining the creative goal.','Integrate spatial constraints and access into artistic design.','small-stage-design'),
    ],
    'cae-iterative-making-and-revision'=>[
        $q('easy','A learner makes a small color study before painting a full poster. What purpose does the study serve?','It lets the learner test color relationships and revise a choice before committing more materials.',['It proves the learner cannot create without a model.','It replaces the need to explain artistic decisions.','It is a final score that ranks the learner against peers.'],'A small test is part of experimentation and can guide informed revision.','Prototype before a larger creative commitment.','color-study'),
        $q('medium','A student says a first sketch must remain unchanged because revision means the idea was wrong. What should the teacher explain?','Revision can refine an idea after the learner sees how materials, audience, or composition affect it.',['Revision is required only when the teacher dislikes the result.','Tell the learner to keep the first sketch unless the teacher requests a change.','Revision means copying a professional example exactly.'],'Revision is a reasoned artistic decision, not a judgment that the initial idea had no value.','Understand revision as part of the creative process.','first-sketch-revision'),
        $q('medium','A sculpture repeatedly tips over. What is the most informative next experiment?','Change one structural feature, test the balance, and compare the result with the original.',['Change every feature at once so the result is surprising.','Add decoration to cover the instability.','Ask another learner to rebuild it without explaining the issue.'],'Changing one feature at a time helps the learner understand how the material and structure affect balance.','Use controlled experimentation in creative problem-solving.','tipping-sculpture'),
        $q('medium','A group receives critique that its performance rhythm is difficult to follow. Which revision plan is best?','Identify the section causing confusion, test a clearer transition, and ask listeners whether the sequence is easier to hear.',['Replace the entire performance without discussing the feedback.','Dismiss the listeners because the group already rehearsed.','Increase volume without checking rhythm or transitions.'],'A targeted test responds to evidence and lets the group evaluate whether the revision improves communication.','Use critique to make and evaluate an artistic revision.','performance-rhythm-feedback'),
        $q('medium','A learner changes a collage after noticing that the focal image is hidden by text. Which explanation shows reflective practice?','The text competed with the focal image, so I moved it to make the main idea easier to see.',['I changed it because the teacher said my original was wrong.','I moved the text because everyone else used a different layout.','I made a change but did not look at the image again.'],'The learner connects a formal choice with audience effect and explains the reason for revision.','Explain how a revision changes meaning or perception.','collage-text-revision'),
        $q('hard','A group has conflicting feedback: some viewers find a dance sequence clear, others do not. What is the strongest next step?','Ask which moments viewers found unclear, compare their observations with the choreography, and test a focused revision.',['Accept only the feedback that supports the group’s first idea.','Change the whole sequence based on a majority vote.','Conclude that viewers should have understood without further evidence.'],'Specific feedback helps locate the issue while preserving the group’s intention and supporting a testable revision.','Synthesize critique before revising collaborative work.','conflicting-dance-feedback'),
    ],
    'cae-performing-arts-interpretation'=>[
        $q('easy','A music group varies dynamics in a repeated section. What question best supports interpretation?','What expressive effect did the change in volume create, and what musical evidence supports that choice?',['Which performer made the fewest mistakes during rehearsal.','Why should every repeated section use the same volume.','Which instrument is most popular with the audience.'],'The question connects a performance choice to an intended effect and audible evidence.','Explain performance choices through musical evidence.','dynamic-contrast'),
        $q('medium','A dance group changes formation during a phrase. Which reflection prompt connects movement and meaning?','How did the change in spacing alter the relationships or focus visible to the audience?',['Which learner stood closest to the teacher.','Why must the formation match a single traditional pattern.','How many steps were in the phrase, without discussing its effect.'],'Space and relationships are movement choices that can shape interpretation.','Analyze movement and spatial choices in performance.','formation-change'),
        $q('medium','A drama group wants a scene to show hesitation without adding dialogue. Which performance choice is most relevant?','Use a deliberate pause or movement change and discuss how the audience may read it.',['Add a costume from an unrelated period.','Increase speaking speed during the silent moment.','Ask the audience to guess without showing a performance choice.'],'Timing and movement can communicate meaning, and the group can test the audience effect.','Use performance elements to communicate an idea.','silent-hesitation'),
        $q('medium','A class is arranging a short musical sequence using sounds contributed by learners. What supports collaboration?','Agree on a shared structure and invite learners to explain how their sound fits the group’s idea.',['Select one learner’s sound and exclude all others.','Have each learner perform separately without listening.','Choose sounds randomly and avoid rehearsal.'],'A shared structure supports individual contribution and coherent collaboration.','Create and interpret collaborative performance.','class-sound-sequence'),
        $q('medium','A performer asks to change a movement because the original gesture is uncomfortable and not essential to the learning goal. What should the teacher consider?','Adapt the movement safely while discussing how the change can retain the intended expressive quality.',['Require the gesture because all performers must move identically.','Remove the learner from the performance.','Change the whole performance without considering the learning intention.'],'An accessible adaptation can preserve the artistic intention while supporting participation.','Adapt performance demands without losing the expressive aim.','gesture-access-adjustment'),
        $q('hard','An audience interprets a performance differently from the creators’ stated intention. How should the teacher guide reflection?','Compare the creators’ intended message with audience observations and identify which performance features shaped each reading.',['Tell the audience its interpretation is wrong because it differs from intention.','Ignore the audience and assess only the creators’ explanation.','Change the performance immediately to please every viewer.'],'Reflection can examine the relationship among intent, performance evidence, and audience response.','Analyze the interaction of intention and reception.','audience-interpretation'),
    ],
    'cae-authentic-arts-assessment'=>[
        $q('easy','A learner’s objective is to explain how texture changes a visual composition. Which assessment is best aligned?','Ask the learner to identify a texture choice and explain its effect using the artwork as evidence.',['Score the learner on how neatly the art table was cleaned.','Ask the learner to name every artist in the textbook.','Rank the artwork by whether the teacher would display it.'],'The assessment directly elicits the stated understanding of texture and effect.','Align assessment with the artistic learning target.','texture-composition-assessment'),
        $q('medium','A portfolio shows several drafts and a final composition. What question best assesses the learner’s process?','Which revision most changed the composition, and what evidence led to that decision?',['Which draft looks most like the teacher’s sample.','How many pages are in the portfolio.','Which learner finished first.'],'Process evidence supports reflection on artistic decisions and development.','Assess artistic process through portfolio evidence.','portfolio-revision-reflection'),
        $q('medium','A dance rubric includes “stage presence” but the taught objective is rhythmic sequence. What should the teacher do?','Score rhythm and sequence as the main criteria and include presence only if it was taught as an objective.',['Keep stage presence as the highest-weight criterion because it is visible.','Remove all criteria and assign one overall impression.','Add a costume score to balance the rubric.'],'The criteria should reflect taught outcomes rather than unrelated presentation preferences.','Keep arts assessment construct-aligned.','dance-rubric-alignment'),
        $q('medium','A learner can explain a design decision but the final artwork has an unfinished edge. How can assessment remain informative?','Use separate criteria for the artistic reasoning and the required technical finish.',['Use the unfinished edge to disregard all process evidence.','Give full credit because the learner explains the idea confidently.','Score the learner only against classmates’ finished work.'],'Distinct criteria make reasoning and technical execution visible without letting one erase the other.','Assess multiple taught dimensions separately.','design-decision-finish'),
        $q('medium','A teacher gives critique only after a final performance, leaving no chance to apply feedback. What would improve the assessment cycle?','Include a formative rehearsal check with one criterion-linked next step before the final performance.',['Keep critique until all performances are complete.','Replace performance with a written quiz.','Give feedback only to learners who ask publicly.'],'Formative feedback can guide improvement while learning is in progress.','Use formative assessment to support artistic growth.','late-performance-feedback'),
        $q('hard','A group project is assessed with one shared grade, although members contributed in different ways. What is the strongest assessment design?','Assess shared collaboration criteria and each learner’s individual evidence of the stated artistic goals.',['Give every member the same score based only on the final display.','Grade the group by the most skilled member’s performance.','Replace the project with individual copying tasks.'],'A combined view can recognize group creation and individual learning evidence.','Assess collaborative product and individual contribution.','group-project-assessment'),
    ],
    'cae-accessible-arts-learning'=>[
        $q('easy','A lesson asks learners to analyze shape and balance, but the only response option is a written paragraph. Which addition improves access?','Allow an annotated drawing or spoken explanation that identifies the same shape and balance evidence.',['Remove shape analysis for learners who choose a different response.','Require the paragraph before accepting any evidence.','Ask a peer to complete the response on the learner’s behalf.'],'Alternative response modes can preserve the visual-analysis competency when writing is not the target.','Provide accessible response options aligned to the arts goal.','shape-balance-response'),
        $q('medium','A classroom display is too high for some learners to examine closely. What should the teacher change?','Provide the images at an accessible height or offer a close-viewing copy while keeping the same analysis task.',['Ask learners to rely on classmates’ descriptions only.','Remove the display from the lesson.','Assess learners on memory of the image rather than observation.'],'Accessible presentation lets learners use the artwork as evidence for the shared task.','Make artistic materials accessible for observation.','high-display-access'),
        $q('medium','A learner cannot use a standard brush comfortably during a painting task. What is a fair next step?','Offer an adapted tool or another application method and assess the intended color or composition choices.',['Complete the painting for the learner.','Replace the arts objective with a written summary.','Score the learner lower for using a different tool.'],'An adapted tool can provide access while preserving the intended artistic evidence.','Adapt tools to support meaningful creation.','adapted-painting-tool'),
        $q('medium','A music activity asks learners to identify repeating rhythm, but the class relies only on a sound that one learner cannot access. What should the teacher add?','Provide an accessible visual or tactile representation of the rhythm while maintaining the pattern-identification task.',['Remove the rhythm goal for the learner.','Ask a peer to identify the pattern for the learner.','Use a faster sound so the pattern is easier to hear.'],'A second representation can support access without changing the rhythm pattern being assessed.','Provide accessible representations of artistic evidence.','rhythm-access-mode'),
        $q('medium','A group critique requires spontaneous public speaking, though the goal is to give evidence-based feedback. Which option supports access?','Allow prepared written, recorded, or partner-shared feedback that names evidence and a next step.',['Remove the feedback goal for learners who prefer not to speak.','Require unprepared speaking because it is the usual format.','Ask the teacher to give all feedback instead.'],'The mode of response is separate from the feedback competency. The teacher can keep the same evidence and next-step criteria while offering a format that lets each learner participate.','Adapt critique participation to the intended competency.','critique-response-access'),
        $q('hard','A teacher adapts a dance sequence for a learner who uses a mobility device. What should guide the adaptation?','Ask the learner, preserve the intended rhythm or movement concept, and assess meaningful contribution in an accessible form.',['Require the learner to copy every standing movement.','Assign the learner a nonparticipating observer role by default.','Change the learning goal to equipment maintenance.'],'Adaptation should reflect learner input and preserve the artistic movement aim while enabling participation.','Design accessible movement participation with learner voice.','mobility-device-dance'),
    ],
    'cae-contemporary-media-literacy'=>[
        $q('easy','A student combines found audio, a photograph, and a digital drawing in one composition. What should the class examine?','How each medium contributes to meaning and how the sources are attributed.',['Which medium is most modern and therefore most valuable.','Whether using several media removes the need for an artistic idea.','How to hide the sources so the composition feels original.'],'Multimedia work involves both meaning-making and responsible attribution.','Evaluate medium and source relationships.','multimedia-composition'),
        $q('medium','A digital work uses an effect that draws attention away from the intended subject. What should the artist test?','Compare the work with and without the effect and decide which version better serves the intended focus.',['Add more effects until the subject becomes visible.','Keep the effect because digital work should appear complex.','Remove the subject and assess only the filter.'],'The medium is a choice; comparison helps determine whether it supports the intended communication.','Evaluate how digital technique shapes audience focus.','digital-effect-focus'),
        $q('medium','A class views a short online video artwork with no context about its creator. What is a responsible discussion opening?','Describe the work, check its source information, and separate observation from unverified interpretation.',['Assume the video is authentic because it appears on an arts channel.','Infer the creator’s identity from style and present the guess as fact.','Discuss an intended meaning as established even though no source identifies the maker.'],'Media literacy includes source awareness and careful separation of observation from inference. Learners can discuss visible and audible features while marking any unsupported context as unknown.','Evaluate contemporary media with source awareness.','online-video-artwork'),
        $q('hard','A learner wants to remix a publicly posted image into a new artwork. What should the teacher ask first?','What is the source and permitted context, how will it be attributed, and how will the learner’s own contribution change its meaning?',['How can the learner remove the creator’s name from the file.','Whether public posting means attribution is unnecessary.','How to reproduce the image exactly before adding a title.'],'The question prompts source attribution and original transformation without assuming every reuse is permitted.','Apply source and attribution reasoning to digital media.','public-image-remix'),
    ],
];
